# Remote MCP resource-server vhost (RFC-112 / ADR-114). Deploy (deploy-player.sh) seds the real
# player domain in and drops this into /etc/caddy/sites/mcp.caddy, then restarts caddy.
#
# WHY: an external AI agent (claude.ai custom connector, Claude Code, Cursor) reaches the platform's
# ONE remote MCP server here, over the public edge, and authenticates as a platform user with their
# OWN bring-your-own model (D6-safe). The MCP container (127.0.0.1:8009) enforces bearer auth
# IN-PROCESS — it verifies every token against the app's tailnet-only /internal/mcp/verify and 401s
# otherwise — so this vhost has **no coming-soon gate**: the auth IS the gate. Unlike the player
# apex, there is no browser app + no cookie flow here; it is a machine-to-machine API surface.
#
# It also serves the PUBLIC RFC 9728 discovery doc /.well-known/oauth-protected-resource (the MCP
# middleware answers it un-authenticated) so a cold client can walk resource → authorization server.
#
# The authorization server itself (DCR / authorize / token / RFC 8414 metadata) lives on the player
# APEX (https://<domain>/api/app/mcp/oauth/* + /.well-known/oauth-authorization-server), reachable
# through player.caddy's OAuth gate-exemption. This vhost is only the RESOURCE.
#
# Rollback: `rm /etc/caddy/sites/mcp.caddy && sudo systemctl restart caddy`.
mcp.player.example.com {
	import hardened
	# Per-site ACME email — info@<player domain> (the deploy seds player.example.com).
	tls info@player.example.com

	# Everything proxies to the MCP server. Streamable HTTP responses can stream (chunked / SSE),
	# so disable response buffering (flush_interval -1) — otherwise a long tool call would buffer.
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8009 {
		flush_interval -1
	}
}
